{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjXbciXW4pf5aEkH2iwd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ptkp5e9oVjzUpUELkwC11S","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"Bar 1 of the line: one dotted half on C3 (second space, F clef) with a stroke through the stem. That is shorthand for six repeated C3 8ths, so it is one of bars 5, 6 or 7. Bar 2 of the line: the same dotted half on C3 with a stroke, so another all-C3 bar. Bar 3 of the line: a G2 quarter tied to a G2 8th, then the repeat sign, then an 8th rest and a quarter rest. That is bar 8. Bar 4 of the line: a G3 8th slurred to a G2 8th, then four G2 8ths. That is bar 9. Bar 5 of the line (the last): a G3 8th slurred to G2, two G2 8ths, then two high 8ths under a slur, G3 and F3. That is bar 10. Bar 8 is preceded by exactly two all-C3 bars, so those are bars 6 and 7. Bar 5 must end the line before, and bar 4 (C2 then C3s) does not appear here. The line therefore holds bars 6–10, not 7–10. The program missed the first shorthand bar.","right":false,"insights":"The copyist writes each bar of repeated C3 8ths as one dotted half with a stroke, so three bars in a row look identical. The start can only be fixed by counting back from bar 8 (tied G2, repeat sign, rests). Counting the barlines before that bar, and expanding the shorthand to eight 8ths per bar's worth, would have caught the missing bar."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5293,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5293},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41692ca9-2742-49a8-a573-b0f14f359aa2","uuid":"7f89a24b-6cbd-45f8-98f5-26007